{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>בַּשָּׂדֶה — on the face of the field</b> — Our Sages said (Ḥullin 72a) that it includes the covering stone (golel) and the side-stone (dofek). And its plain sense: on the face of the field, where there is no tent (ohel), the corpse defiles by contact — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 19:16). And golel and dofek, according to the Sages, are not derived from the verse, but it is a halakhah, and the nazirite does not shave on account of it, and one is not liable on account of it for defilement of the sanctuary and its holy things. But <b>עַל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה</b> according to our Sages is as its plain sense: that one touched a slain body slain by a sword (ḥalal ḥerev) that fell on the face of the field and there is nothing tenting over it. And because of its superfluity they made a midrash of it, as they said in tractate Nazir in the chapter Kohen Gadol (Nazir 53b): \"And whatsoever touches עַל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה\" — this refers to one who tents over the corpse, for one who tents over it is touching upon the face of the field. And for them, \"בַּחֲלַל חֶרֶב\" (slain by a sword) is written deficient [without] the vav: \"וּבַחֲלַל חֶרֶב.\" And because it says \"פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה\" and does not say \"הַשָּׂדֶה,\" Rabbi Yishmael derived from it (Ḥullin, ibid.) further to exclude a fetus in its mother's womb, for it is a swallowed [concealed] defilement and is not upon the face of the place. And it says \"בַּחֲלַל חֶרֶב\" because the verse spoke of the present case, and the same law applies to a corpse [killed by] a stone or a fist. And it mentioned ḥalal and met (slain and dead) in contrast to animals, for those properly slaughtered are pure and the nevelah (carcass) defiles. And our Sages (Nazir 53b) made a midrash of it because of its superfluity, to say that the sword is like the corpse (ḥalal) — for the verse came only to equate the corpse and the sword, to say that it becomes a father of fathers of defilement like the corpse itself, and defiles a person and vessels with seven-day defilement. And what appears from the sugyot in the Mishnah and in the Gemara is that it defiles by contact (maga) and by carrying (masa) like a corpse, but it is not like it to defile in a tent (ohel). Perhaps they found a restriction in it from the verse regarding the tent: <b>אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת בְּאֹהֶל</b> — that it does not apply only to the person himself. And if the sword that became defiled by a corpse were to defile in a tent, the priests would be forbidden in all the houses, for in all of them there is a defiled sword and it would defile them in the tent. And so it further appears in the Gemara (ibid. 54a) that a sword that touched a corpse does not defile a person to require him to be sprinkled on the third and seventh [days], even though this person who touched it is a father of defilement. And this would be because in the second verse that says \"וְהִזָּה עַל הָאֹהֶל\" (Numbers 19:18), it is written: \"וְעַל הַנֹּגֵעַ בַּעֶצֶם אוֹ בֶחָלָל אוֹ בַמֵּת\" (and upon one who touched a bone or a corpse or a dead person), and it does not say \"אוֹ בַּחֲלַל חֶרֶב\" (or a slain body slain by a sword). And if so, the sword is like a corpse to defile by contact with seven-day defilement, but not to defile in a tent and not to require sprinkling, and the nazirite does not shave on account of it, and the priest is not warned against contact with it at all. And this is what is close and apparent from the words of our Sages. And we, in our sins, are defiled by the exile, and we have no knowledge of the purity of the sanctuary until a spirit from on high is poured upon us, and HaShem sprinkles pure water upon us and we become pure. Amen, so may it be His will speedily in our days."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:10.937891857Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}